WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.220
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:03.930
JOHN DOLHUN: I've
done these demos

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:05.820
in front of live audiences.

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:09.390
I remember one time I did
one demo in front of alumni.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:13.080
I forget what it was,
but it was a failure.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.240
And this one little kid--

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:18.150
he was only about four years
old-- he raised his hand.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:22.920
He said, you win some
and you lose some.

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:24.450
SARAH HANSEN: Today
on Chalk Radio,

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:29.880
we're talking about the course
5.310, Laboratory Chemistry.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.800
This is a course
that not only teaches

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.760
students about hands-on
science, but also life.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.090
Because working in a lab
is a lot like baking--

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:44.280
you can follow recipes, but
your cake might still collapse.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.740
I'm your host Sarah Hansen.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:50.040
In this episode, we're
joined by two MIT educators

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:52.620
who have helped shape
this standout course.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:54.150
They'll tell us
more about what it

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:57.420
looks like to support students
with various academic interests

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.930
as they develop lab skills,
overcome setbacks, and apply

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:05.545
their studies to real-world
problems like water quality.

00:01:05.545 --> 00:01:07.170
JOHN DOLHUN: I'm John
Dolhun, currently

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:11.700
Director of the Undergraduate
Chemistry Teaching Labs at MIT.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.260
SARAH HEWETT: I'm
Sarah Hewett and I

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:17.102
am a technical instructor in
the Chemistry Department at MIT.

00:01:17.102 --> 00:01:18.810
SARAH HANSEN: For many
of their students,

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:22.440
5.310 is the first time they
step foot in a college level

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:27.030
chemistry lab, and that can
be a memorable experience.

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:29.040
JOHN DOLHUN: The first
thing you would see

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.820
are lines of student
coat lockers.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:35.100
And that's the first thing
that students encounter

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.750
when they enter the lab.

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:39.850
It's here where they
would put their bags,

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:43.690
and they store their lab
coats and their goggles.

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:46.660
And you would soon see
walking out into the lab

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:49.840
that it's divided into 12 bays.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:55.750
Each bay has 6 to 7 fume
hoods and desk space

00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:59.500
for up to 14 students.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:02.350
SARAH HANSEN: And lots
and lots of windows--

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:05.925
something I may have
read a bit too much into.

00:02:05.925 --> 00:02:07.300
SARAH HEWETT: You
can correct me.

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:10.449
I always thought it was because
in case there's an explosion,

00:02:10.449 --> 00:02:13.060
they want all the windows
to blow out instead

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:15.080
of the walls of the building.

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:16.370
JOHN DOLHUN: An-- [LAUGHS]

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:17.620
SARAH HANSEN: Maybe I'm wrong.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:18.662
JOHN DOLHUN: --explosion.

00:02:18.662 --> 00:02:20.638
[LAUGHS]

00:02:20.638 --> 00:02:22.180
SARAH HANSEN: John
went on to explain

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:28.300
that yes, you would see windows,
but explosions, not so much.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:30.430
Anyway, let's get back
to what you actually

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:33.530
would see in this lab.

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:35.780
JOHN DOLHUN: We have some
really neat instruments that

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:38.090
the students can
operate hands-on,

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:42.200
exactly what the students
would experience in industry--

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:46.220
Cary 60 UV-Vis spectrometers,
state of the art

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:48.920
density meters and
refractometers,

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:52.100
and one of the really
exciting instruments

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:56.000
is an inductively coupled
plasma mass spectrometer.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:58.340
There are only two
of these at MIT.

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:02.750
And this instrument is
used exclusively in 5.310,

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:06.860
and the students actually
use that to test their river

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:10.760
samples to determine the
concentration of 29 different

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:15.330
elements down to the
part per trillion level.

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:17.450
So it's pretty exciting.

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:20.030
SARAH HANSEN: The river sample
testing is part of what makes

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:22.160
5.310 unique.

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:25.250
Students get to take their
lab work right into Boston's

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:27.140
own Charles River.

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:27.890
SARAH HEWETT: Yes.

00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:31.550
The most exciting experiment, I
think, for most of the students

00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:33.590
is the Ellen Swallow
Richards Lab.

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:35.750
So the students actually
go down to the river

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:37.310
and take water samples.

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:39.920
And then they get to analyze
those samples back up

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:43.010
in the lab for dissolved
oxygen, phosphate concentration,

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:44.395
and a bunch of other elements.

00:03:44.395 --> 00:03:45.770
So they get to
learn a little bit

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:48.830
about environmental chemistry,
and analytical chemistry,

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:50.540
and different
techniques that we use

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:51.707
for those types of analysis.

00:03:51.707 --> 00:03:53.623
And the students really
like that because it's

00:03:53.623 --> 00:03:55.340
very applicable to
the Charles River

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:57.560
and to the Boston environment.

00:03:57.560 --> 00:03:59.783
And they can see it
and say, oh, wow, I'm

00:03:59.783 --> 00:04:01.700
actually doing something
that's kind of useful

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:03.050
and something that--
they always hear

00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:04.970
about how dirty the
Charles River used to be,

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:06.553
and they're actually
kind of surprised

00:04:06.553 --> 00:04:09.535
when they do the lab now about
how good the water quality is.

00:04:09.535 --> 00:04:10.910
SARAH HANSEN: You
may have caught

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:13.850
that Sarah and John referred
to this as the Ellen Swallow

00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:15.380
Richards Lab.

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:18.529
It's a namesake with
significant meaning.

00:04:18.529 --> 00:04:21.769
JOHN DOLHUN: When I
wrote this lab in 2010,

00:04:21.769 --> 00:04:24.830
I actually called it
the Charles River Lab.

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:27.860
The reason I wrote the lab was
to introduce an environmental

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:33.500
component into the 5.310
Laboratory Chemistry course.

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:36.290
And sometime after
I had wrote the lab,

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:40.700
I actually was walking
through the halls at MIT,

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:45.590
and somehow I ended up in
the lobby of Building 2.

00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:49.610
And I looked up and I saw
this plaque and this photo

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:52.550
of this woman on the wall.

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:55.010
And I started reading
it and it said,

00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:59.810
this was the first female
student ever admitted to MIT

00:04:59.810 --> 00:05:03.350
who graduated in 1873.

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:05.630
It was Ellen Swallow
Richards, who

00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:07.880
was an industrial
safety engineer

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:10.190
and environmental chemist.

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:12.470
And it was really
her work, I think,

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:17.420
in 1887 which led to the
first state water quality

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:19.470
standards in the nation.

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:22.100
And that was very
significant, and that kind of

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:26.510
moved me to rename the Charles
River Lab the Ellen Swallow

00:05:26.510 --> 00:05:29.050
Richards Lab.

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:31.510
SARAH HANSEN: The groundwork
laid by Ellen Swallow Richards

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:35.050
has never been more important
in a way that feels particularly

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:39.928
relevant for my own community
and likely for some of yours.

00:05:39.928 --> 00:05:41.470
SARAH HEWETT: One
of the local issues

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:43.390
that has come up in
the Cambridge area

00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:46.870
and around the country is
the issue of PFAS, which

00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:49.900
are per- and
polyfluoroalkyl substances,

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:52.540
which are man made chemicals
that have been around

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:54.190
for less than 100 years.

00:05:54.190 --> 00:05:55.720
So they're relatively
new, but they

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:58.930
encompass things like
Teflon and other chemicals

00:05:58.930 --> 00:06:02.590
like that that are involved
in non-stick coatings for food

00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:05.350
and also in fire suppression
technology because they're

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:07.270
really flame resistant,
and stain resistant,

00:06:07.270 --> 00:06:09.040
and stick resistant.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:12.940
So now they're being
detected in water supplies

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:14.800
and in the food
chain, and scientists

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:17.920
are pretty worried about
their impact on human health.

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:20.465
And they're still not entirely
sure what all of the impacts

00:06:20.465 --> 00:06:22.090
are, but there's been
a lot of research

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:24.700
recently into detecting
these in the environment

00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:29.432
and trying to figure out what
their impact is on health.

00:06:29.432 --> 00:06:30.890
SARAH HANSEN: As
you might imagine,

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:33.980
most students aren't ready to go
out into the field on Day 1 one

00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:35.720
of 5.310.

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:38.750
The first step is learning
how to be safe in the lab.

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:41.420
And learning how to interact
with chemicals and instruments

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:44.167
in the lab can be daunting.

00:06:44.167 --> 00:06:46.250
JOHN DOLHUN: They're coming
in and they're afraid.

00:06:46.250 --> 00:06:50.490
Some of them have never, ever
been in a wet lab before.

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:53.610
You've got to make them feel
really comfortable, even

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:55.260
though they might
be a fourth year

00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:58.350
student at MIT, graduating.

00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:01.110
SARAH HEWETT: The really fun
thing about 5.310 is that there

00:07:01.110 --> 00:07:03.090
isn't really a typical student.

00:07:03.090 --> 00:07:06.790
We get students from all types
of majors that take the course.

00:07:06.790 --> 00:07:09.030
So it's a requirement
for medical school

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:11.490
and it's a requirement for the
chemical engineering major,

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:14.340
so we get a bunch of
chemical engineers

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:16.740
and some med school
or pre-med students.

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:20.460
But they all come from majors
all across the Institute,

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:21.730
which is really fun.

00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:23.665
So even though a lot
of them have not taken

00:07:23.665 --> 00:07:25.290
a chemistry lab maybe
since high school

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:28.950
or ever, they have a lot of
science knowledge and knowledge

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:31.587
of other things that is
always kind of interesting.

00:07:31.587 --> 00:07:33.420
Sometimes after the
lectures they'll come up

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:36.003
and say, oh, this reminds me of
something from my other class.

00:07:36.003 --> 00:07:38.820
And that's how I learn a lot,
too, just from talking to them

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:40.823
and hearing about
their experiences.

00:07:40.823 --> 00:07:42.490
They actually end up
helping each other.

00:07:42.490 --> 00:07:44.400
So some of them who say, oh,
I've worked in a biology lab,

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:46.192
I might have more
experience with pipetting

00:07:46.192 --> 00:07:48.390
can help students who
maybe have not taken

00:07:48.390 --> 00:07:51.738
a lab class in many years.

00:07:51.738 --> 00:07:54.280
SARAH HANSEN: For beginner and
experienced chemistry students

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:57.460
alike, 5.310's labs
are a challenge.

00:07:57.460 --> 00:07:59.770
That means John and Sarah
have thought carefully

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:02.980
about what kinds of support
their students might need.

00:08:02.980 --> 00:08:07.030
JOHN DOLHUN: Many, many
schools use a lab manual,

00:08:07.030 --> 00:08:12.250
and they use experiments that
are very clearly published out.

00:08:12.250 --> 00:08:15.730
And the students go through
maybe 12 experiments

00:08:15.730 --> 00:08:19.360
in a semester and they're
very systematic in terms

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:21.820
of the types of experiments
that are there--

00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:24.730
the traditional experiments.

00:08:24.730 --> 00:08:28.090
But with MIT, some
of the experiments

00:08:28.090 --> 00:08:32.200
are multi-day,
multifaceted experiments

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:37.090
that are actually put together
and can be quite challenging.

00:08:37.090 --> 00:08:41.890
A typical experiment could
be 50 to 60 pages long.

00:08:41.890 --> 00:08:44.410
In terms of helping them
deal with frustration,

00:08:44.410 --> 00:08:47.410
I believe in divide and conquer.

00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:50.200
For example, one of
the things that's

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:53.020
very difficult, and
challenging, and stressful

00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:55.990
might be writing
up the lab report.

00:08:55.990 --> 00:08:59.720
That might take them
10 to 15 hours of time.

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:02.530
So we teach them
how to break this up

00:09:02.530 --> 00:09:06.820
to deal with small
frustrations systematically.

00:09:06.820 --> 00:09:09.400
Do small parts of it
early on and don't

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:11.780
wait till the end to
do the whole thing.

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:15.370
It's kind of like them working
on tiny anthills instead

00:09:15.370 --> 00:09:16.960
of a big mountain.

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:19.540
And so, by forming
these good habits,

00:09:19.540 --> 00:09:21.130
they'll soon be
able to deal with

00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:24.280
mountain-sized frustration.

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:27.718
I'm a product of MIT
and it works for me.

00:09:27.718 --> 00:09:29.260
SARAH HANSEN: In
addition to building

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:31.570
the perseverance
chemistry requires,

00:09:31.570 --> 00:09:33.700
both students and
instructors are also

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:36.730
actively cultivating
curiosity and making

00:09:36.730 --> 00:09:39.760
mistakes in the process.

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:41.470
JOHN DOLHUN: Students
love diapers.

00:09:41.470 --> 00:09:43.450
And as soon as you
pull a diaper out,

00:09:43.450 --> 00:09:48.190
that totally gets them
relaxed and loosens them up.

00:09:48.190 --> 00:09:51.610
The polymer demo is all
about sodium polyacrylamide,

00:09:51.610 --> 00:09:53.530
and that's the diaper polymer.

00:09:53.530 --> 00:09:58.030
That's the chemical in the
diapers that absorbs the water

00:09:58.030 --> 00:10:00.190
at 800 times its weight.

00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:02.050
[LAUGHS]

00:10:02.050 --> 00:10:05.500
When I do this demo, I
usually call on a volunteer.

00:10:05.500 --> 00:10:08.530
And unbeknownst
to the volunteer,

00:10:08.530 --> 00:10:11.560
I've already put some
powder in the cup.

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:14.500
And I start pouring
water in a cup

00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:18.130
and I ask the volunteer
to tell me when to stop.

00:10:18.130 --> 00:10:22.090
When they tell me to stop, I
put a plastic cover on the cup,

00:10:22.090 --> 00:10:26.170
and turn it over, and then
I put it on their head.

00:10:26.170 --> 00:10:28.630
Usually I ask them
to hold it so they

00:10:28.630 --> 00:10:32.470
can assume any
responsibility for that water

00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:34.780
in the cup coming out.

00:10:34.780 --> 00:10:38.560
But usually, nothing comes
out because the polymer has

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:41.470
absorbed all the water, right?

00:10:41.470 --> 00:10:45.130
You get this beautiful globular
polymer inside this cup.

00:10:45.130 --> 00:10:48.670
And you knock it out into
a dish, and you can see it.

00:10:48.670 --> 00:10:55.120
But I remember when I did this
demo at one of the MIT parents'

00:10:55.120 --> 00:10:57.250
visiting weekends.

00:10:57.250 --> 00:11:01.120
And there were about 70 to
80 people in the audience,

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:04.300
and I asked for a
volunteer to come down.

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:08.260
So he came down, but
he didn't tell me

00:11:08.260 --> 00:11:11.150
soon enough to stop pouring
the water in the cup.

00:11:11.150 --> 00:11:14.890
So I just kept going
till he said stop, right?

00:11:14.890 --> 00:11:18.550
So I covered the cup
up very carefully,

00:11:18.550 --> 00:11:20.650
turned it over on
this hard plastic,

00:11:20.650 --> 00:11:22.930
and I put it on his head.

00:11:22.930 --> 00:11:25.480
And the polymer
and all the water,

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:27.880
when I pulled the card
out, it all came out

00:11:27.880 --> 00:11:30.280
and it messed up his hairdo.

00:11:30.280 --> 00:11:34.940
It was a gooey, gooey mess,
and the audience just loved it.

00:11:34.940 --> 00:11:38.710
I mean, we had to clean him
up with paper towels in front

00:11:38.710 --> 00:11:39.430
of everybody.

00:11:39.430 --> 00:11:45.790
So there's often unexpected
fun in these live demos.

00:11:45.790 --> 00:11:46.540
SARAH HEWETT: Yes.

00:11:46.540 --> 00:11:49.240
I think that's one of the
fun parts about the lab,

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:52.050
is that not everything works.

00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:53.433
It doesn't work
always for demos.

00:11:53.433 --> 00:11:55.600
But even in the lab you can
have two students follow

00:11:55.600 --> 00:11:57.490
the exact same
procedure, and sometimes

00:11:57.490 --> 00:11:58.877
it just doesn't come out.

00:11:58.877 --> 00:12:01.460
Or they'll make one little error
that they didn't even notice,

00:12:01.460 --> 00:12:02.418
and then it won't work.

00:12:02.418 --> 00:12:05.080
And so that is
definitely something

00:12:05.080 --> 00:12:06.580
that we deal with
a lot in the lab--

00:12:06.580 --> 00:12:08.788
dealing with that frustration
and having the students

00:12:08.788 --> 00:12:10.870
say, well, trying to
figure out what went wrong

00:12:10.870 --> 00:12:12.220
and how to fix it for next time.

00:12:12.220 --> 00:12:15.065
But that is a big part of
what happens in the lab,

00:12:15.065 --> 00:12:16.690
and a big lesson I
think that comes out

00:12:16.690 --> 00:12:19.000
that's very unrelated to
chemistry but very important

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:19.630
for life.

00:12:21.902 --> 00:12:24.110
SARAH HANSEN: Another
important aspect of this course

00:12:24.110 --> 00:12:28.310
is its emphasis on
science communication.

00:12:28.310 --> 00:12:31.160
JOHN DOLHUN: A few years
ago, we looked at the course

00:12:31.160 --> 00:12:34.880
and we saw the necessity of
writing and for the students

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:38.540
to be able to communicate their
work to a general audience

00:12:38.540 --> 00:12:40.790
of non-scientists.

00:12:40.790 --> 00:12:43.160
By working with
writing, rhetoric,

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:46.070
and professional
communication instructors,

00:12:46.070 --> 00:12:48.680
students learn how to
write and present the paper

00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:50.840
to a general audience.

00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:54.110
SARAH HEWETT: We came up with
a bunch of different topics

00:12:54.110 --> 00:12:57.890
that are related to water
quality, and the Charles River,

00:12:57.890 --> 00:13:01.190
and all of the data that
we gather for that lab.

00:13:01.190 --> 00:13:03.890
Students can choose which
genre they would like to write.

00:13:03.890 --> 00:13:05.420
So some examples
that we have are,

00:13:05.420 --> 00:13:07.940
there's a hospital memo
about mercury levels in fish

00:13:07.940 --> 00:13:10.100
that we measure with
our mercury analyzer.

00:13:10.100 --> 00:13:13.340
So they can use the data to
make arguments about that.

00:13:13.340 --> 00:13:17.450
There are policy memos
for the government talking

00:13:17.450 --> 00:13:20.210
about why it's important to
limit phosphate concentration

00:13:20.210 --> 00:13:21.980
and pollution in the water.

00:13:21.980 --> 00:13:23.660
It challenges them
to think about how

00:13:23.660 --> 00:13:25.400
to communicate science
in these different ways.

00:13:25.400 --> 00:13:26.942
And the writing
instructors have been

00:13:26.942 --> 00:13:28.700
amazing in helping
them learn how to write

00:13:28.700 --> 00:13:30.260
in these different styles.

00:13:30.260 --> 00:13:34.005
I never learned to write science
from a writing perspective--

00:13:34.005 --> 00:13:36.630
it was always just in my science
classes from other scientists.

00:13:36.630 --> 00:13:39.590
And so I would recommend
it to any other instructor

00:13:39.590 --> 00:13:42.947
to bring in writing help if you
have it because it carries over

00:13:42.947 --> 00:13:44.780
to their lab reports,
too, because they have

00:13:44.780 --> 00:13:46.970
all these skills of how
to read their own work,

00:13:46.970 --> 00:13:50.742
and how to assess it for
clarity, and all those things.

00:13:50.742 --> 00:13:52.450
SARAH HANSEN: One
thing about MIT faculty

00:13:52.450 --> 00:13:55.180
that we love is their commitment
to sharing their teaching

00:13:55.180 --> 00:13:58.190
materials with everyone.

00:13:58.190 --> 00:14:02.150
I was curious what motivated
Sarah and John to do this.

00:14:02.150 --> 00:14:03.920
SARAH HEWETT: Sharing
the course materials

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:07.520
is a really helpful resource
to other instructors

00:14:07.520 --> 00:14:10.130
because I know I've benefited
from talking to people that I

00:14:10.130 --> 00:14:12.380
went to graduate school with
about what they're doing,

00:14:12.380 --> 00:14:14.520
and going to conferences, and
learning from other people.

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:16.370
And so the more that
we can share and make

00:14:16.370 --> 00:14:19.130
available these
materials, the more it

00:14:19.130 --> 00:14:20.930
can help other
instructors, and also

00:14:20.930 --> 00:14:22.550
to introduce more
people to chemistry.

00:14:22.550 --> 00:14:24.550
If they have their one
experience of a chemistry

00:14:24.550 --> 00:14:27.308
course, then maybe they
can go online and see,

00:14:27.308 --> 00:14:28.850
hey, maybe this
other place is taught

00:14:28.850 --> 00:14:30.410
in a different way that's
way more interesting,

00:14:30.410 --> 00:14:31.700
or, I think that
that's something

00:14:31.700 --> 00:14:32.750
that I might like to do.

00:14:32.750 --> 00:14:34.700
So I think it benefits
everybody to be

00:14:34.700 --> 00:14:37.040
more open with what's going on.

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:39.800
JOHN DOLHUN: OpenCourseWare
has such a good reputation

00:14:39.800 --> 00:14:42.980
in terms of people all over
the world that actually

00:14:42.980 --> 00:14:45.710
turn to OpenCourseWare
to open a course

00:14:45.710 --> 00:14:49.700
and actually learn the
material from that course.

00:14:49.700 --> 00:14:52.640
I've had students--
freshman advisees--

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:55.070
who have come in who've
learned everything that they

00:14:55.070 --> 00:14:58.280
know from OpenCourseWare.

00:14:58.280 --> 00:15:01.340
It's quite dramatic when
you have a student come in

00:15:01.340 --> 00:15:04.380
and they literally can
test out of every subject,

00:15:04.380 --> 00:15:06.830
but they've learned
all of those subjects

00:15:06.830 --> 00:15:10.173
from watching OpenCourseWare.

00:15:10.173 --> 00:15:11.840
SARAH HANSEN: Before
we go, I also asked

00:15:11.840 --> 00:15:14.310
Sarah and John what they
would like to ask you,

00:15:14.310 --> 00:15:17.120
our listeners, about teaching
laboratory chemistry.

00:15:17.120 --> 00:15:17.870
JOHN DOLHUN: Yeah.

00:15:17.870 --> 00:15:20.180
I think I'd be
curious to know what

00:15:20.180 --> 00:15:22.610
demos some of the
other educators

00:15:22.610 --> 00:15:25.580
do in their laboratory
chemistry courses,

00:15:25.580 --> 00:15:28.760
and also what types of
non-traditional labs

00:15:28.760 --> 00:15:30.250
they're running.

00:15:30.250 --> 00:15:32.000
SARAH HEWETT: I think
my favorite question

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:34.010
to ask other lab
instructors is, what

00:15:34.010 --> 00:15:36.020
is the purpose of teaching lab?

00:15:36.020 --> 00:15:38.090
So a lot of people
have different answers

00:15:38.090 --> 00:15:40.370
to that, which is always
kind of interesting.

00:15:40.370 --> 00:15:43.220
And then, also, another question
that I've been thinking about

00:15:43.220 --> 00:15:45.230
is how to prepare
students for what they're

00:15:45.230 --> 00:15:47.870
going to be doing next, whether
it's going into industry,

00:15:47.870 --> 00:15:50.060
or academia, or pre-med.

00:15:50.060 --> 00:15:52.940
What can we do in
the lab to help them

00:15:52.940 --> 00:15:56.097
with that, whether it's
chemistry or related or not?

00:15:56.097 --> 00:15:57.930
SARAH HANSEN: If you
have insights to share,

00:15:57.930 --> 00:16:00.740
please get in touch with me
the link in our show notes.

00:16:00.740 --> 00:16:03.590
I'll be sure to pass your
ideas along to John, Sarah,

00:16:03.590 --> 00:16:05.390
and other listeners.

00:16:05.390 --> 00:16:08.420
If you're interested in learning
more about laboratory chemistry

00:16:08.420 --> 00:16:11.630
or remixing Sarah and John's
open educational resources

00:16:11.630 --> 00:16:15.410
in your own teaching, head on
over to our MIT OpenCourseWare

00:16:15.410 --> 00:16:16.680
website.

00:16:16.680 --> 00:16:18.510
Thank you so much for listening.

00:16:18.510 --> 00:16:20.390
Until next time, signing off.

